\section{S-expressions}\label{ch30-s-expressions}

\texttt{SExpr} is a value containing an atom or a list.
\texttt{SExpr.\allowbreak{}atom} /
\texttt{SExpr:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}atom} and \texttt{list}
construct expressions. \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}sexpr} reads one
expression; \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}sexpressions} reads multiple.
\texttt{dump(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} produces a readable
representation. Semicolon comments, nested lists, quoted atoms, and
\texttt{\textbackslash{}n}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}r},
\texttt{\textbackslash{}t}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}"},
\texttt{\textbackslash{}\textbackslash{}} escapes are supported. Invalid
input raises an exception.

\texttt{ast\_\allowbreak{}to\_\allowbreak{}sexpr} and
\texttt{sexpr\_\allowbreak{}to\_\allowbreak{}ast} bridge the existing
runtime AST. An AST leaf is represented by
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}tag value)\allowbreak{}}; an inner node by
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}tag children.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
Consequently an S-expression with one atom argument converts to an AST
leaf, and bare atoms convert to
\texttt{leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}"atom"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} /
\texttt{leaf!"atom"}. Use \texttt{SExpr} itself when that distinction
matters. Lists used as e-graph terms require an atom operator head.

\section{E-graphs and equality
saturation}\label{ch30-e-graphs-and-equality-saturation}

An \texttt{EGraph} stores hash-consed e-nodes in union-find e-classes.
\texttt{unite} establishes equality; \texttt{rebuild} restores
congruence. For example, merging \texttt{a} and \texttt{b} also makes
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}f a)\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}f b)\allowbreak{}} equivalent. Public IDs remain
usable after merges; \texttt{find} returns their current representative.

Rules are S-expressions:

\begin{code}
(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
(rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
(= (add ?a ?b) (add ?b ?a))
\end{code}

\texttt{?name} denotes a pattern variable. A repeated variable must
match the same e-class, not necessarily the same textual expression.
Quoted \texttt{"?name"} is data. RHS variables must be bound on the LHS.
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}=\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} lhs rhs)\allowbreak{}}
is accepted too. Rules match their LHS and introduce their RHS; equality
itself is symmetric, but matching in the reverse orientation requires
another rule.

C++:

\begin{code}
dsl::EGraph graph;
auto root = graph.add("(mul (add a 0) 1)");
auto rules = dsl::sexpr_rules(R"(
    (rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
    (rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
)");
auto report = graph.saturate(rules);
assert(report.saturated());
assert(graph.extract(root).dump() == "a");
\end{code}

D:

\begin{code}
auto graph = new EGraph;
auto root = graph.add("(mul (add a 0) 1)");
auto rules = sexpr_rules(`
    (rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
    (rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
`);
auto report = graph.saturate(rules);
assert(report.saturated());
assert(graph.extract(root).dump() == "a");
\end{code}

\texttt{matches(\allowbreak{}pattern)\allowbreak{}} returns
\texttt{EMatch} records (\texttt{root}, \texttt{bindings}). The optional
third argument to \texttt{saturate} observes each successful rule
application. It may run more than once for a binding across iterations.
\texttt{when\_\allowbreak{}equal(\allowbreak{}a,\allowbreak{} b,\allowbreak{} callback)\allowbreak{}}
calls a user rewriter with the graph and representative only when the
IDs are equivalent. Thus a user can respond to equality independently of
saturation.

\texttt{SaturationLimits\allowbreak{}} bounds iterations, retained
nodes, and total applications. The result reports \texttt{Saturated},
\texttt{IterationLimit}, \texttt{NodeLimit}, or \texttt{MatchLimit}. A
bounded run retains valid partial results. \texttt{extract} selects a
minimum-node finite expression even when the graph contains cycles.
\texttt{extract\_\allowbreak{}ast} converts that representative back to
the runtime AST.

\section{Graphs, terms and reduction}\label{ch30-graphs-terms-and-reduction}

\texttt{DirectedGraph} provides stable node and edge handles, ordered
adjacency, mutation, traversal, shortest paths, topological sorting,
strongly connected components, and DOT output. It supports cycles,
parallel edges, and isolated nodes. Label callbacks adapt arbitrary
payloads for DOT rendering.

\texttt{Term} represents first-order function applications and
variables, with S-expression parsing, structural matching, simultaneous
substitution, and occurs-checked unification.
\texttt{TermRewriteSyste\allowbreak{}m} applies validated rules in
insertion order using outermost or innermost traversal. Normalization
reports whether it reached normal form or exhausted its step budget.

\texttt{TermGraph} represents finite acyclic terms with shared
subexpressions. Reduction redirects every incoming edge of a matched
node to its replacement, so a shared redex is reduced once for all its
occurrences. Callers retain the returned root and explicitly collect
unreachable nodes. The general graph supports cycles; term graph
reduction requires acyclic terms. Step budgets limit applications, not
memory or recursion depth.

See Chapter 37 (Chapter 37) for both language APIs, examples, DOT
rendering, root-handle rules, and the distinction between tree rewriting
and shared graph reduction.

\section{Abstract serialization and
deserialization}\label{ch30-abstract-serialization-and-deserialization}

The library defines protocols and forwarding adapters, with no
prescribed wire syntax. A user backend supplies:

{\def\LTcaptype{none} % do not increment counter
\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3333}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3333}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3333}}@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
Operation
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
C++
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
D
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\endlastfoot
Serialize &
\texttt{backend.\allowbreak{}serialize(\allowbreak{}value,\allowbreak{} output)\allowbreak{}}
& same \\
Deserialize &
\texttt{backend.\allowbreak{}template deseria\allowbreak{}lize\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}input)\allowbreak{}}
&
\texttt{backend.\allowbreak{}deserialize!T(\allowbreak{}input)\allowbreak{}} \\
\end{longtable}
}

\texttt{Serializer\textless{}\allowbreak{}Backend\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
/ \texttt{Serializer!Backe\allowbreak{}nd} and \texttt{Deserializer}
borrow that backend and preserve its return type and error handling.
Inputs and outputs can be any user type; they need not be binary
streams. \texttt{SerializerBacken\allowbreak{}d} and
\texttt{DeserializerBack\allowbreak{}end} expose protocol checks.

\section{Abstract byte I/O}\label{ch30-abstract-byte-io}

\texttt{IO\textless{}\allowbreak{}Backend\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} /
\texttt{IO!Backend} borrows a backend implementing:

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \texttt{read(\allowbreak{}mutable byte buf\allowbreak{}fer)\allowbreak{} -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} transferred cou\allowbreak{}nt};
\item
  \texttt{write(\allowbreak{}const byte buffe\allowbreak{}r)\allowbreak{} -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} transferred cou\allowbreak{}nt}.
\end{itemize}

C++ buffers are
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}span\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}byte\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
/
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}span\textless{}\allowbreak{}const std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}byte\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
D buffers are \texttt{ubyte[]} /
\texttt{const(\allowbreak{}ubyte)\allowbreak{}[]}. Zero read means EOF.
Short transfers are valid. \texttt{read\_\allowbreak{}exact} and
\texttt{write\_\allowbreak{}all} loop over short transfers; premature
EOF, invalid counts, or a non-progressing write raise an exception.
\texttt{seek} and \texttt{tell} are available when the backend supplies
them. Backend lifetime is user-managed.

\section{Static regexes and binary
streams}\label{ch30-static-regexes-and-binary-streams}

\texttt{StaticRegex\textless{}\allowbreak{}"pattern"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
/ \texttt{StaticRegex!"pat\allowbreak{}tern"} compiles a Thompson NFA at
compile time. Matching treats input as bytes, including NUL and bytes
above \texttt{0x7f}. Supported syntax:

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  literals and escaped literals;
\item
  \texttt{.\allowbreak{}} (any byte), classes/ranges, negated classes;
\item
  grouping
  \texttt{(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
  alternation \texttt{|}, repetition \texttt{*}, \texttt{+}, \texttt{?};
\item
  beginning/end assertions \texttt{\textasciicircum{}}, \texttt{\$};
\item
  \texttt{\textbackslash{}xHH}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}0},
  \texttt{\textbackslash{}n}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}r},
  \texttt{\textbackslash{}t}.
\end{itemize}

There are no capture groups, backreferences, shorthand classes such as
\texttt{\textbackslash{}d}, or counted repetitions. \texttt{matches}
requires a whole-input match. \texttt{prefix} returns the longest
matching end offset from a position; \texttt{search} returns the
earliest matching span, choosing the longest match at that position.

\texttt{BinaryStream} owns a byte buffer and cursor.
\texttt{read(\allowbreak{}count)\allowbreak{}} returns a span/slice;
\texttt{next\textless{}\allowbreak{}Regex\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
/ \texttt{next!Regex(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} consumes a regex
token, leaving the cursor unchanged on failure. Empty tokens raise an
exception. \texttt{write} appends bytes.
\texttt{read\_\allowbreak{}integer\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
/ \texttt{read\_\allowbreak{}integer!T} and
\texttt{write\_\allowbreak{}integer} use explicit
\texttt{ByteOrder.\allowbreak{}Little/\allowbreak{}Big}
(\texttt{:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}} in C++).
\texttt{load(\allowbreak{}io,\allowbreak{} limit)\allowbreak{}} reads
until EOF within a size bound;
\texttt{save(\allowbreak{}io)\allowbreak{}} writes through the abstract
I/O protocol.

\section{Derive a visitor from an
AST}\label{ch30-derive-a-visitor-from-an-ast}

Handlers are registered with
\texttt{on\textless{}\allowbreak{}"tag"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}}
/ \texttt{on!"tag"(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}}. A handler may take
the node, or a compile-time \texttt{ASTTag} followed by the node.
\texttt{derive\_\allowbreak{}visitor(\allowbreak{}schema,\allowbreak{} handlers.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
derives the accepted tag set from a runtime AST and constructs a
template-specialized dispatch table. Missing handlers are rejected
immediately; visiting a tag outside the schema is rejected.
\texttt{visit} visits one node, and \texttt{walk} traverses in
\texttt{VisitOrder.\allowbreak{}Preorder} or \texttt{Postorder}.

For compile-time schema validation, C++ uses a type-level AST:

\begin{code}
auto schema = dsl::ast_schema<"add">(dsl::ast_schema<"num">());
auto visitor = dsl::compile_visitor(schema,
    dsl::on<"add">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}),
    dsl::on<"num">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}));
\end{code}

D can use the actual runtime AST type at CTFE:

\begin{code}
enum schema = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(0));
auto visitor = compile_visitor!schema(
    on!"add"((ref ASTNode n) {}),
    on!"num"((ref ASTNode n) {}));
\end{code}

This compiles handler dispatch and validates a static schema; it is not
a runtime JIT compiler. Visitor callbacks supply the semantic
operations.

\section{Built-in preprocessor and optional
Lua}\label{ch30-built-in-preprocessor-and-optional-lua}

\texttt{Preprocessor} supports object/function macros, balanced
arguments, recursive macro suppression, backslash-newline continuation,
include directives, and conditional blocks. Macro expansion respects
strings, character literals, and C-style comments. Conditional
directives are \texttt{\#if}, \texttt{\#ifdef}, \texttt{\#ifndef},
\texttt{\#elif}, \texttt{\#else}, \texttt{\#endif}. Conditions support
integer constants, \texttt{defined}, \texttt{!}, comparisons,
\texttt{\&\&}, and \texttt{||}. Other operations are \texttt{\#define},
\texttt{\#undef}, \texttt{\#include}, \texttt{\#error},
\texttt{\#lua code}, and \texttt{\#script language\allowbreak{} code}.

This is a DSL preprocessor with the documented subset, not a complete
C/C++ preprocessor: token-pasting, stringification, variadic macros, and
arithmetic in \texttt{\#if} are not implemented. \texttt{include} is a
user callback returning source for a path; \texttt{script} returns
source for a language and code string. Generated script output is
macro-expanded. Expansion/include depths are bounded.

For actual Lua embedding, C++ defines
\texttt{MUDSL\_\allowbreak{}ENABLE\_\allowbreak{}LUA}, supplies the Lua
include/link flags, and assigns
\texttt{pp.\allowbreak{}script = dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}lua\_\allowbreak{}script\_\allowbreak{}hook(\allowbreak{}state)\allowbreak{}}.
D builds with \texttt{-d-version=MuDSL\allowbreak{}Lua} (LDC),
\texttt{-version=MuDSLLu\allowbreak{}a} (DMD), or
\texttt{-fversion=MuDSLL\allowbreak{}ua} (GDC), links Lua 5.3/5.4, and
assigns
\texttt{pp.\allowbreak{}script = lua\_\allowbreak{}script\_\allowbreak{}hook(\allowbreak{}state)\allowbreak{}}.
The user owns/configures the Lua state. Each directive returns a string.
The bridge restores the Lua stack after success or failure. For example:
\texttt{\#lua return '(\allowbreak{}= a b)\allowbreak{}'}.

\section{Explicit context-free
grammar}\label{ch30-explicit-context-free-grammar}

\texttt{CFG} has named nonterminals, token terminals, and productions.
Its Earley parser handles left recursion, epsilon productions and
unordered alternatives. It returns an AST for the first discovered
derivation of an ambiguous grammar, or a farthest token position and
expected terminals. The user's lexer supplies the input token sequence.

Grammar S-expression notation:

\begin{code}
(grammar
    (start Expr)
    (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term))
    (rule Term ("number")))
\end{code}

Unquoted RHS symbols are nonterminals; quoted symbols are literal
tokens. Each RHS list is an alternative;
\texttt{(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is epsilon.
\texttt{sexpr\_\allowbreak{}grammar} loads this notation and validates
references. Alternatively, construct \texttt{CFGSymbol} and
\texttt{CFGRule} values, or use \texttt{CFGTerminal},
\texttt{CFGNonterminal}, \texttt{CFGProduction} and
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}cfg} templates. The resulting AST tags are
production names; terminal leaves use the tag \texttt{token}.

\section{Enum reflection}\label{ch30-enum-reflection}

C++ retains the complete embedded \texttt{magic\_\allowbreak{}enum}
public API described in Chapter 29. D provides native reflection through
\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}name}, \texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}values},
\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}names}, \texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}entries},
\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}count}, checked casts/indexing, adjacency,
compile-time dispatch/fusion, flags and enum-indexed containers. It has
no numeric search-range restriction. \texttt{EnumOptions} and
\texttt{EnumNames} UDAs customize flags, prefixes and display names. D's
native formatting and bitwise operators replace the C++ stream/operator
integrations.

\section{Verification}\label{ch30-verification}

From the MuDSL directory:

\begin{code}
cmake -S tests -B build/mudsl-tests -DMuDSL_TEST_LUA=ON
cmake --build build/mudsl-tests
ctest --test-dir build/mudsl-tests --output-on-failure
ldc2 -unittest -main MuDSL.d -of=mudsl-test
./mudsl-test
\end{code}

The C++ test suite includes
\texttt{test\_\allowbreak{}language\_\allowbreak{}facilities.\allowbreak{}cpp}
and an optional native Lua bridge test. D's tests are embedded in its
single module.
